<LegalLayout title="Privacy Policy | Motive11" description="What Motive11 records, what it does not, and what happens to your information.">
<p class="eyebrow">Motive11</p>
  <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
  <p class="standfirst">
    Most privacy policies are written to cover the company. This one is written to tell you what
    actually happens. The short version is that this website keeps a record of how people use it, ties that record to you only once you give me your email, never does any of it for visitors in Europe, and the information that really matters is the material you hand over once you become a client.
  </p>
  <p class="updated">Last updated November 7, 2026</p>

  <section>
    <h2><span class="num">1</span>The short version</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>This website keeps its own record of visits:</strong> the pages you view, what you click, and where you came from. It sets one cookie of its own to do that. There is no advertising network, no retargeting pixel, and no data broker.</li>
      <li>The record is anonymous until you give me your email, by booking a call, submitting a form, or clicking a link in an email I sent you. From then on, your visits to my own websites are tied to you. Nothing follows you anywhere else on the internet.</li>
      <li><strong>If you are in Europe, none of this happens.</strong> No cookie, no visit record, and nothing tied to you.</li>
      <li>The information worth caring about most is what you send me directly: emails, files, documents, and access to your systems during an engagement.</li>
      <li>That material goes through AI providers as part of how the work gets done. The <a href="/ai-policy">AI Policy</a> explains exactly how, and it is worth reading before the rest of this page.</li>
      <li>Nothing is sold, rented, or handed to advertisers. Ever. There is no version of this business where that makes sense.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2><span class="num">2</span>What this website records</h2>
    <p>
      I want to know who is reading this site and what brought them here, so it keeps a visit record
      of its own. This is what goes into it:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Pages you view,</strong> with the page's title, the site that linked you here, and any campaign tags on the link (the "utm" part of an address).</li>
      <li><strong>What you click</strong> on the site, by the label on the link or button, and any link you follow to another site.</li>
      <li><strong>When you start a form,</strong> meaning the first time you click into one. Not what you type into it.</li>
      <li><strong>Your email, when you give it to me:</strong> when you book a call, submit any form on the site, or click a link in an email I sent you. When you book, your name, your email, and your answers are also stored with the booking and added to my calendar, and for a paid booking your card details go straight to Stripe.</li>
      <li><strong>Roughly where you are and how you are connected:</strong> your city, region, and country, and the name of your internet provider, which Cloudflare works out from your connection. I never store your IP address in this record.</li>
      <li><strong>Your device:</strong> your browser and operating system, your screen and window size, your language, and your time zone, plus how long a page was open, how far you scrolled, and how fast it loaded. That is how I tell a person from a program pretending to be one.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      To connect one visit to the next, the site sets <strong>one cookie</strong> of its own, called
      m11_vid. It holds a random number and nothing else, and it lasts a year. My server sets it, not a
      script on the page, for two reasons: no script on the page can read it, and Safari erases
      anything a page's own scripts save after seven days without a visit, while deciding to hire
      someone usually takes longer than that. On the booking page, Stripe's card form can set its own
      cookies for fraud prevention when you pay.
    </p>
    <p>
      Until you give me your email, the record is anonymous: a random number, a city, and a list of
      pages. Once you do, everything that browser does on my own properties is tied to you, before and
      after. My own properties are this site, markszymanski.co, my short links at mjs.link, and the
      emails I send. If you use a phone and a laptop, those are two records with the same email. I do
      not track you on any site I do not own, and I do not buy or match data from anyone else.
    </p>
    <div class="callout">
      <p>
        <strong>Nobody in Europe is tracked or identified.</strong> If you visit from the EU, the EEA,
        the UK, Switzerland, Turkey, or anywhere else in Europe, the site sets no cookie, keeps no
        visit record, never reads your device details, and never ties a visit to you, and any cookie
        from an earlier visit is cleared. Your visit shows up only in the anonymous Fathom counts
        below. That is also why there is no cookie banner: where the law asks for a yes first, I
        don't keep the record at all.
      </p>
    </div>
    <p>
      What is never recorded: what you type into a form (other than your email when you submit it),
      your mouse movements, and recordings of your session.
    </p>
    <p>
      Traffic is also counted with <strong>Fathom Analytics</strong>, which is cookieless by design.
      It does not set cookies, does not fingerprint you, does not retain IP addresses, and does not
      follow you to other sites. What it produces is counts: pages viewed, rough referrer, rough
      country, and device type. None of it identifies a person.
    </p>
    <p>
      Your IP address is seen by <strong>Cloudflare</strong>, which hosts this site and the booking system, because serving you a web page is impossible without it. The booking page also uses Cloudflare's bot check, and Stripe when a booking is paid. That is standard infrastructure
      logging, it is not used to build a profile of you, and I do not have access to it as an
      identifiable record.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2><span class="num">3</span>When you contact me</h2>
    <p>
      Contact happens by email or through the booking page on this site. My personal site, markszymanski.co, is a separate site with its own policies. If you email me, I have your email, your name, and whatever you chose to
      put in the message. That sits in my mailbox the way any business correspondence does.
    </p>
    <p>
      I use what you send to answer you and, if it goes somewhere, to scope and quote the work. I do
      not add you to a marketing list because you emailed me. The one list I run is my weekly
      newsletter, and you're on it only if you sign up for it yourself, with your first name and
      email. Every issue has an unsubscribe link.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2><span class="num">4</span>When you become a client</h2>
    <p>
      This is the part that actually matters, and it is not really about the website at all. During
      an engagement you hand over real material: documents, spreadsheets, customer data, email
      threads, analytics, credentials, and access to systems you own.
    </p>
    <p>
      That material is used to do your project and for nothing else. It is not published, not shared
      with other clients, and not used to build something I sell to someone else. Where the work becomes a case study, the <a href="/terms">Terms of Service</a> cover it under "Showing the work", and credentials and your customers' personal information are never part of it.
    </p>
    <div class="callout">
      <p>
        <strong>Your material is processed by AI providers.</strong> That is central enough to how
        the work is produced that it has its own page. The <a href="/ai-policy">AI Policy</a> covers
        what is transmitted, which provider controls are enabled, what is never sent, how to exclude
        specific material, and what you own at the end. It is part of the
        <a href="/terms">Terms of Service</a> and is incorporated by reference.
      </p>
    </div>
    <p>
      If your project involves personal information about <em>your</em> customers, which is common,
      it is handled as your data and not mine. I do not use it for any purpose outside your project,
      and if you need a separate data processing agreement in place, ask and we will sign one.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2><span class="num">5</span>What never gets collected</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Card numbers.</strong> Payments run through <strong>Stripe</strong>. Card details go to Stripe directly and I never see or store them. What I get back is the fact that an invoice was paid, and the billing name and email attached to it.</li>
      <li><strong>Government identifiers.</strong> Social security numbers, tax identifiers, licenses, passports. There is no reason for this business to hold any of it, so it does not.</li>
      <li><strong>Credentials sent to AI providers.</strong> Passwords, keys, and access tokens are used to operate your systems and are not transmitted to AI providers. This is stated in the AI Policy too, because it is worth saying twice.</li>
      <li><strong>What you type, beyond your email.</strong> The visit record never captures what you type into a form, your mouse movements, or a recording of your session. Your email is kept only when you submit it.</li>
      <li><strong>Anything from a tracking network.</strong> No advertising pixels, no retargeting, no data brokers, and no third-party audience tools.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2><span class="num">6</span>Who else touches your information</h2>
    <p>
      A one-person business still runs on software built by other people. These are the services that
      can come into contact with your information, and what each one is for:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Google Workspace</strong> hosts my email, so any correspondence with me lives there.</li>
      <li><strong>Stripe</strong> processes payments and holds the billing record.</li>
      <li><strong>Bento</strong> sends my newsletter and holds the list, so if you sign up, your first name and email are stored there. Bento records whether you open an issue and which links you click.</li>
      <li><strong>Postmark</strong> sends transactional email, meaning things like confirmations and notifications. It is not a marketing platform and is never used as one.</li>
      <li><strong>Cloudflare</strong> hosts this site and much of what gets built, and stores the visit record described above in a database on my Cloudflare account.</li>
      <li><strong>Fathom Analytics</strong> counts page views, anonymously, as described above.</li>
      <li><strong>AI providers</strong>, currently Anthropic as the main one, process material during an engagement. See the <a href="/ai-policy">AI Policy</a>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Each of these has its own terms and its own privacy policy, and those govern what they do with
      data they hold. I can tell you what Motive11 does and which controls are enabled. I cannot make
      promises on a third party's behalf, and you should be suspicious of anyone who does.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2><span class="num">7</span>How long things are kept</h2>
    <p>
      Working files and correspondence for a project are kept for the life of the engagement and for
      a reasonable period after it, because clients come back and asking you to re-send everything is
      a bad experience. Invoices, contracts, and financial records are kept as long as tax and
      business law requires, which is several years.
    </p>
    <p>
      An anonymous visit record, one never tied to an email, is deleted automatically once that
      browser has gone 180 days without visiting. A record tied to your email is kept until you ask me
      to delete it.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you want your material deleted sooner, say so. Anything I am not legally required to keep
      gets removed, and I will tell you plainly what has to stay and why.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2><span class="num">8</span>Your choices</h2>
    <p>
      You can ask me what information I hold about you, ask for it to be corrected, ask for a copy,
      or ask for it to be deleted, your visit record included. There is no portal and no form for this. Email
      <a href="mailto:hello@motive11.com">hello@motive11.com</a> and you will get a real answer from
      the person who actually has the files.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you are somewhere with specific statutory data rights, including the EU and UK under GDPR or
      California under the CCPA, those rights apply to you and the way to exercise them is the same
      email. Motive11 does not sell personal information, so there is nothing for you to opt out of
      on that front.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2><span class="num">9</span>Security</h2>
    <p>
      Accounts are protected with strong unique credentials and multi-factor authentication, devices
      are encrypted, and client credentials are stored in a password manager rather than in a
      document or an email thread.
    </p>
    <p>
      No one can honestly promise perfect security, and a policy that claims it is telling you
      something false on the page where it should be most careful. What I can promise is that if
      something involving your material goes wrong, you hear it from me quickly and directly.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2><span class="num">10</span>Children</h2>
    <p>
      This is a business to business service. It is not directed at children, and information about
      children is not knowingly collected. If you believe a child has sent me information, email me
      and it will be deleted.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2><span class="num">11</span>Changes</h2>
    <p>
      This page gets updated when what I actually do changes, and the date at the top moves with it.
      If a change is significant and you are a current client, you get told directly rather than left
      to notice a new date on a web page.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2><span class="num">12</span>Contact</h2>
    <p>
      Motive11 LLC is a Pennsylvania limited liability company based in Pittsburgh, and this policy
      is governed by Pennsylvania law. Questions about any of it are welcome, including the awkward
      ones, and the answer will be a straight one.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="mailto:hello@motive11.com">hello@motive11.com</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</LegalLayout>
